On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:34:20 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Consider the commit plan approach I've been mulling over. Sure, I could
> commit that to CLAUDE.md - but why? I think that's more of a skill than
> a global constraint, right?

Yes. Anthropic's own guidance draws the line in the same place. The
skills docs say to make a skill "when a section of CLAUDE.md has grown
into a procedure rather than a fact". The best-practices page says to
test each CLAUDE.md line with "Would removing this cause Claude to
make mistakes?", and to put workflows that are "only relevant
sometimes" in skills [a][b].

So the split I would use here is:

- CLAUDE.md holds what is true whichever approach is used: commit
  style, patch scope, self-contained series, quoting, "Own the loop".
  These are the interface's contract.
- A skill holds a procedure for meeting that contract: commit-plan,
  spec-first, or whatever comes next.

The commit plan as you described it in "Re: [RFC] how to spec work in
a way that's readable for humans" [1] is a procedure. Write the
messages first, then fill in the code. My reply in the same thread
[2] adds four rules to it, and those are procedure too. None of it
belongs in CLAUDE.md.

> Research what other people are doing in this area.

Each item is marked [F] for something I read in the source, or [I]
for my inference.

- [F] Packaged workflows are the norm now, and they ship as skills or
  plugins rather than as CLAUDE.md text. Superpowers (Jesse Vincent)
  is a plugin of skills chained as brainstorm, worktree, write plan,
  execute plan, TDD, review [c]. GitHub's Spec Kit is spec-first as a
  set of commands: constitution, specify, plan, tasks, implement [d].
  Both are whole "build" implementations you could swap in or out.

- [F] Plugins can be loaded for one session without installing them,
  with `claude --plugin-dir <dir>`. A --plugin-dir copy overrides an
  installed plugin with the same name, and enabledPlugins can turn one
  off per scope [e].

- [F] One practitioner uses a "router" skill: one entry point takes
  the variant as an argument and dispatches to it [f]. That is your
  interface with swappable implementations, done inside one skill.

- [F] For A/B on real work, the common practice is cheap. Run the
  same ask from the same commit in two worktrees, one per approach,
  and compare the diffs. Anthropic's best-practices page recommends
  worktrees for "isolated experiments" [b]. The best public example
  is Felipe Fontoura's comparison [g]. He gave one brief to Spec Kit,
  Superpowers, BMAD and OpenSpec, with the same commit and model, and
  scored each on a 13-item acceptance check plus time and cost. Spec
  Kit took 473s, cost $2.84 and wrote 690 lines of spec. Superpowers
  took 40s and cost $0.33. He calls the result anecdotal, since each
  tool ran once.

- [I] Nobody I found keeps a formal eval for this day to day. People
  try a variant on a real task, look at the result, and keep or drop
  it. That matches what you want.

We have done this once already. src/katai/bowling runs the same kata
as claude_elixir_bowling and claude_elixir_bowling_plan, with prompts
bowling-001-big-steps.md and bowling-002-incremental.md. prompts/ and
plans/ are an earlier plan-first implementation that was never named
as one.

> Thinking more like a programmer, it's designing an interface. We might
> have a build function in the interface, which can be implemented either
> commit-plan or spec-first. The general interface stays the same "build
> this new feature" but the implementation of it is swappable.

I would write the interface down. It should be one short file that
says what any build must take and produce:

- Input: an ask, usually a message on the list.
- Output: a series posted to the list that meets CLAUDE.md, and a
  reply citing it.
- How it is judged: the list itself. We can count how many versions
  a series needed, how many review points it drew, and whether it
  landed as first posted. notmuch can count all of that without
  asking the model. That fits "Own the loop", where the model's
  account of how well it did never counts.

Each implementation is then a skill that meets that contract:
build-commit-plan, build-spec-first. Both set disable-model-
invocation, so Claude never picks one for itself; you choose it.

Where they live is the part that needs your decision. In b34672a7d9c5,
"claude: Move skills out of the main dir", you took skills out of
.claude/skills. The reason was the bootstrap problem: they shift the
sand under Claude while it works. Every skill there loads in every
session, so an experimental one is always live. I see three options:

1. .claude/skills/build-<impl>/. This is the cheapest option, but it
   brings the bootstrap problem back for experiments, which are the
   skills most likely to change.

2. One plugin per implementation, e.g. claude/build/commit-plan/ and
   claude/build/spec-first/, loaded with --plugin-dir. A session sees
   only the implementation you launched it with, and nothing is live
   by default. lab-process-mail could take the choice as a flag, and
   the A/B is then two passes with different --plugin-dir values.
   [I] The cost is that plugin skills get a namespace prefix, so the
   two must share a plugin name to share an invocation. I have not
   checked how that behaves when both are loaded at once.

3. A router skill /build <impl>. There is one entry point and the
   implementations sit in files beside it. It has the same bootstrap
   problem as 1, plus a dispatch table to maintain.

I recommend 2. It is the only one that keeps experiments out of every
session by default, which is the problem b34672a7d9c5 solved. It also
makes the choice explicit at launch, where you can see it, and not
something the model selects.

This overlaps with "[RFC] documenting monorepos" [3]. The interface
file is process documentation, so it should go wherever doc/process/
ends up.

If you pick a location, I will post a series. It will add the
